MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

/guides was the thinnest section left: 21 pages at a median of 175 words,
the shortest at 137, eleven of them carrying two sections of one paragraph
each. These are the authority pages — a 137-word guide called "How long to
acclimate hardwood in the GTA" does not rank, does not get quoted by an
answer engine, and does not persuade a homeowner who has just been told by a
contractor that five days is enough.

Stage 7 refused to write local colour for 32 municipalities because that
would be inventing knowledge about places Franco knows and I do not. Guides
are the opposite case: acclimation equilibrium, subfloor flatness tolerance,
radiant commissioning sequences, nail-down versus glue-down failure modes,
herringbone layout and waste, nosing geometry. General trade knowledge,
checkable against NWFA practice and the Ontario Building Code, making no
claim about any job this shop did. The line held in both directions, which
is the point of having one.

All 20 guides now run 502-775 words, median 552, written to the section's
existing voice. Each one names the failure mode rather than only the method:
what squeaks and why, what cups and why, which quote to walk away from.

The stair-code and nosing guides interpolate OBC_LIMITS from data/obc.ts
instead of restating rise, run, tread depth, nosing projection, handrail and
guard dimensions as prose — the same discipline data/services.ts already has
for prices.

That caught a live drift: carpet-to-hardwood-stairs-gta claimed a 13-step
flight "typically runs $5,000-$11,000" as a typed literal, while the
estimator banded across every city multiplier computes $6,100-$9,800. The
guide now interpolates the computed figure, so the guide and the estimator
cannot disagree.

338 tests pass. The suite caught two paragraphs I wrote under its own
80-character stub threshold, which is the rule working. 393 pages, audit
clean, no new URLs.

Still thin: /answers at a median of 258 across 40 pages, and the nine
/portfolio/[slug] pages at 237.

